# DEFAULT DENY. This repo is publishable; the workspace it runs against is not.
#
# The engine reads the retail Sacred Gold install at runtime and ships none of
# it. That property is only true as long as nothing but hand-written source
# lands here, so this file is an allowlist, not a blocklist. A blocklist fails
# open: anything you forget to name gets committed. This fails closed.
#
# To track a new file type, add an explicit "!" line and think about what is in
# it first. Extracted terrain blobs, .pak contents, screenshots, traces, save
# games and decompiler output are all derived from commercial media and must
# never appear in this history.

# 1. Deny everything, but still descend into directories.
*
!*/

# 2. Allow hand-written engine source and project files only.
!.gitignore
!.gitattributes
!LICENSE
!*.gd
!*.gdshader
!*.uid
!*.tscn
!*.tres
!*.md
!project.godot
# Named individually rather than as *.sh: this is the only shell script the
# engine has any business carrying, and it holds no path into the install --
# it resolves its own directory and lets Sacred.find_install() do the rest.
!run.sh
# E1's authored scenario harness. The manifest defines scenarios by input
# grammar and expected authoritative state -- it names no retail bytes. The
# runner is plain Python against the public engine CLI. Both are hand-written;
# private captures and reference checkpoints stay in the workspace's tmp/.
!tools/scenarios.json
!tools/verify_scenarios.py

# 3. Belt and braces: regenerable or retail-derived, named so the intent
#    survives an edit to the rules above.
.godot/
export/
data/
*.pck
tmp/

# graft's local graph cache — regenerable, not committed (run `graft build`).
/graft/
